client-triage
PC 侧客户端经代理访问境外服务的排障方法与工具。

This product includes software developed as part of the client-triage project,
a personal troubleshooting repository for client-side (Windows + Chrome) issues
behind a router-side proxy.

==============================================================================
Third-party components
==============================================================================

The components below are used as **runtime dependencies / external tools**,
unless the entry explicitly says otherwise. Their own licenses govern them.
One project is vendored into this repository; it is listed first, and its
full source credential (upstream URL, commit SHA, clone date, update
procedure) lives in third-party/devtools-protocol/UPSTREAM.md.

- Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) -- devtools-protocol
  https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/devtools-protocol
  https://chromedevtools.github.io/devtools-protocol/
  Licensed under the BSD-3-Clause License.
  Used for: the authoritative protocol definition behind tools/cdp.js -- the
  script talks to a running Chrome instance over the DevTools WebSocket
  endpoint and reads Log.entryAdded, Runtime.consoleAPICalled,
  Runtime.exceptionThrown, Network.loadingFailed and Network.responseReceived
  events to turn a vague symptom ("the page only shows an input box") into a
  concrete console error.
  Distribution: **yes** -- the protocol definition files (json/browser_protocol.json,
  json/js_protocol.json) plus the upstream LICENSE and README are vendored
  verbatim under third-party/devtools-protocol/ at commit
  95b1af69d6a20161dc3558f8e38366b7fa7a27d1 (2026-10-19). Not one byte of
  upstream content was modified. It is a read-only reference: no script in
  tools/ requires anything from that directory.
  The full index of vendored projects, and the reasoning for each, is in
  third-party/README.md.

- Chrome / Chromium
  https://www.chromium.org/Chromium/Chromium/
  Licensed under the BSD-3-Clause License (Chromium authors).
  Used for: the browser under diagnosis. It provides the
  `--remote-debugging-port` entry point that tools/cdp.js attaches to, and it
  is the component whose Local Network Access (LNA) check conflicts with the
  router-side fake-IP address space -- see docs/LNA-FAKE-IP.md.
  Distribution: **no** -- not vendored, not redistributed.
  Chrome is a trademark of Google LLC; this project is not affiliated with,
  endorsed by, or sponsored by Google LLC.

- Node.js
  https://nodejs.org/
  Licensed under the MIT License.
  Used for: tools/cdp.js and tools/clear-chrome-region.js. Both scripts rely only
  on Node built-ins (global fetch, global WebSocket, fs, path) and declare no
  third-party npm dependencies.
  Distribution: **no** -- not vendored, not redistributed.


